<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honda Tadakatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1548">1548</a>–<a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honda Tadakatsu was one of the four great generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, famed for fighting in dozens of battles without being seriously wounded. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> domain with 100,000 koku, where he rebuilt the castle and laid out the castle town at the mouth of the Ibi and Kiso rivers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu.jpg" alt="Honda Tadakatsu" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait-Honda-Tadakatsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Honda Tadakatsu, also called Honda Heihachirō, was born on 17 March 1548 at Kuramae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a>, the eldest son of Honda Tadataka, of one of the oldest families of hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. He fought his first battle at Washizu Fort in 1560 beside his father. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a> rose in Mikawa, he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a> sect for Jōdo-shū and stayed loyal to Ieyasu, helping to suppress the rising. In 1566, aged 19, he was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatamoto">hatamoto</a> with command of 54 horsemen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tadakatsu won distinction at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Battle of Anegawa</a> in 1570, where he charged alone against the advancing Asakura army, and in 1572 at Hitokotosaka his antler helmet and his spear Tonbokiri drew the enemy's notice as he held off the units of Baba Nobuharu. At Mikatagahara in 1573 he commanded the left wing and organised the cover of the Tokugawa retreat. At Nagashino in 1575 he led a rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and helped stop the breakthrough of Naitō Masatoyo's men towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s camp.</p><p>After the Honnō-ji Incident of 1582 he helped escort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> from Sakai back to Mikawa through bands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> hunters. In the Komaki campaign of 1584 he rode out with a handful of men to challenge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s host across the Shōnai River. He commanded Tokugawa forces against the Hōjō in 1590, taking Tamanawa and helping to take Iwatsuki and Hachigata, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 he broke into the camps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro">Shimazu Yoshihiro</a> and Ukita Hideie until his horse Mikuni-Guro was shot under him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tadakatsu retired in 1609, handing the headship of the clan to his son Tadamasa, and died in his sleep in December 1610. His daughter Komatsuhime married Sanada Nobuyuki, and his grandson Tadatoki married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senhime">Senhime</a>. He grew estranged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> as it turned from a military to a civil government. Tradition holds that he took part in 57 campaigns and was never seriously wounded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> called him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> among samurai. His spear Tonbokiri, made by Fujiwara Masazane, is counted among the Three Great Spears of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Honda Tadakatsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Kuwana to his famous general <b>Honda Tadakatsu</b> in 1601 with 100,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuwana-domain">Kuwana Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Okazaki, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.17&amp;year=1588" aria-label="Okazaki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8028 899 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Honda Tadakatsu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8478 1169h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8478 1169h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.96,137.17&amp;year=1588">What was here in 1588? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
